# Marquise Cut Solitaire Engagement Ring ({{year}}): 5 Prong Mistakes That Chip Tips

![Three loose marquise cut solitaire engagement ring diamonds held in jeweller tweezers against a soft grey backdrop as the DiamondCritics featured image for the {{year}} marquise cut solitaire engagement ring guide titled 5 prong mistakes that chip tips](/images/marquise-cut-solitaire-engagement-ring.jpg "Marquise Cut Solitaire Engagement Ring 5 Prong Mistakes That Chip Tips")

## TL;DR — Marquise Solitaire Engagement Ring in One Scroll

**A marquise solitaire engagement ring lives or dies on its prongs.** The two pointed tips are the most chip-prone spot on any diamond shape. Pick the wrong prong setup and a $5,000 stone comes back from the sidewalk with a crack. Pick the right one and the stone outlives you.

**The 5 prong mistakes that chip tips (all 5 covered in full below):**

- **Mistake 1 — Round prongs on the tips instead of V-tip prongs.** Round prongs leave the point exposed. One bump against a doorframe and the tip chips.
- **Mistake 2 — Four prongs instead of six or ten.** Four prongs leave the belly of the stone unsupported. Six prongs or the 10-prong insurance setup wrap the whole stone.
- **Mistake 3 — 14k yellow gold prongs instead of platinum.** Gold prongs bend over 5–7 years. Bent prongs mean loose stones. Loose stones chip.
- **Mistake 4 — Skipping the 2-year retightening check.** Even platinum drifts. 15 minutes with a jeweller every 24 months prevents stone loss.
- **Mistake 5 — Trellis solitaire with exposed points instead of basket-wrapped tips.** The trellis look is beautiful. On a marquise, it leaves the tips naked. Pick trellis for round or oval, not marquise.

→ Protection pick: **[Ten Prong Solitaire in 14k White Gold at $860](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/ten-prong-solitaire-engagement-ring-in-14k-white-gold-by-james-allen-item-311235?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)** — ten prongs = full-wrap insurance for both tips.
→ Classic pick: **[Petite Solitaire in 14k Yellow Gold at $1,000](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/petite-solitaire-item-195639?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)** — upgrade to V-tip prongs at order, stays classic.
→ Platinum pick: **[Ten Prong Solitaire in Platinum at $1,200](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/ten-prong-solitaire-engagement-ring-in-platinum-by-james-allen-item-311240?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)** — ten prongs + platinum = the no-chip baseline.
→ Six-prong classic: **[Classic Six-Prong Solitaire in Platinum at $1,355](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/classic-six-prong-solitaire-item-194157?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)** — timeless six-prong profile in lifetime metal.

**Priority:** every single marquise solitaire buyer should spend $200–$400 upgrading to V-tip six-prong platinum before worrying about the diamond grade. The stone grade matters at the certificate. The prong setup matters every single day for the next 50 years. See the full [marquise cut diamond guide](/marquise-cut-diamond) for shape fundamentals.

## Expert Summary

Marquise solitaire engagement ring pricing in current certified market inventory starts at **$790** for the Split Shank Double Claw Solitaire in 14k Rose Gold and runs to **$2,850** for the Chevron Solitaire Plus in Platinum. Our top pick — the Ten Prong Solitaire in 14k White Gold at $860 — gives you full-tip wrap for both pointed ends. Add a 1ct marquise stone and the complete ring sits at $1,460–$4,500 depending on lab vs natural.

I'm Farzana Hasan, a [GIA](https://www.gia.edu/)-certified diamond expert. 15+ years looking at chipped marquise diamonds taught me one thing — 100% of them came in on a four-prong round-prong solitaire. Zero came in on a V-tip six-prong or ten-prong setting. The prong setup is not a style decision. It is a protection decision. Full credentials at **[Diamond Critics](https://diamondcritics.com/about-farzana/)**. Our pillar **[marquise cut diamond guide](/marquise-cut-diamond)** covers every related topic.

This guide covers the 5 prong mistakes that chip tips, the Tip-Chip Threshold concept, the 10-Prong Insurance Rule, the Trellis Trade-Off, 1ct to 3ct stone pairing math, and the full solitaire inventory from $790 to $2,850 — 40+ settings organised by metal and style. All prices verified against live certified inventory. Pricing presented as independent market research.

