</tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Hand-Engraved Solitaire</strong></td><td>Platinum</td><td><a href="https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/hand-engraved-solitaire-engagement-ring-in-platinum-item-203289?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">$2,245</a></td><td>Hand-engraved platinum. Heirloom profile.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Solitaire with Pavé Basket (JA)</strong></td><td>14k Yellow Gold</td><td><a href="https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/solitaire-engagement-ring-with-pave-basket-in-14k-yellow-gold-by-james-allen-item-311230?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">$2,260</a></td><td>Pavé basket under the stone. Hidden sparkle from below.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Criss Cross Solitaire (JA, 14k YG by JA)</strong></td><td>14k Yellow Gold</td><td><a href="https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/criss-cross-solitaire-by-james-allen-item-311132?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">$2,320</a></td><td>Mid-tier criss-cross. Trellis variant.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Criss Cross Solitaire (JA)</strong></td><td>Platinum</td><td><a href="https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/criss-cross-solitaire-engagement-ring-in-platinum-by-james-allen-item-311128?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">$2,445</a></td><td>Trellis in platinum. Platinum metal + trellis style.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Engraved Solitaire Plus Diamond</strong></td><td>Platinum</td><td><a href="https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/engraved-solitaire-plus-diamond-engagement-ring-in-platinum-item-196471?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">$2,595</a></td><td>Engraved platinum + diamond accents.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Solitaire with Pavé Basket (JA)</strong></td><td>Platinum</td><td><a href="https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/solitaire-engagement-ring-with-pave-basket-in-platinum-by-james-allen-item-311234?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">$2,635</a></td><td>Pavé basket in platinum. Hidden halo-like sparkle.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Chevron Solitaire Plus (CEILING)</strong></td><td>Platinum</td><td><a href="https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/chevron-solitaire-plus-engagement-ring-in-platinum-item-196310?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">$2,850</a></td><td>Solitaire ceiling. Chevron shoulders + platinum.</td></tr></tbody></table>

**Verdict:** 50+ solitaire settings run from $790 to $2,850 for a straight centre-only mount. The $860 Ten Prong is the top pick for every marquise buyer who cares about keeping both tips intact. The $1,200 platinum Ten Prong is the lifetime pick. The $1,355 Classic Six-Prong Platinum is the heirloom-classic pick. Every ring above accepts a marquise centre — the stone shape is selected separately at order.

## 5 Prong Mistakes That Chip Marquise Tips

**The 5 mistakes below are responsible for 100% of chipped marquise tips I have graded in 15+ years.** Every single one. Fix them at the order stage for $200–$400 extra and the stone stays intact for life.

### Mistake 1 — Round Prongs on the Tips Instead of V-Tip Prongs

Standard round prongs sit above the tip, pressing down. The two sharp points of the marquise stick out past the metal. One bump against a doorframe, car door, or kitchen counter catches the exposed point. The tip chips. Re-cut cost: $2,000–$25,000 depending on carat.

V-tip prongs wrap the tip from both sides, forming a protective V around each pointed end. The upgrade cost is $200–$400 at the order. The chip risk reduction is 60–70%.

<table class="dc-custom-table"><thead><tr><th>Prong Type at Tip</th><th>Tip Protection</th><th>Chip Risk</th><th>What this means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Round single prong</strong></td><td>None at the point</td><td>Very High</td><td>Avoid on marquise. Default on cheap settings — ask every retailer.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>V-tip prong</strong></td><td>Full wrap of the point</td><td>Low</td><td>Our baseline. Non-negotiable for marquise.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Double claw at tip</strong></td><td>Two prongs per point</td><td>Low</td><td>Alternative to V-tip. Also wraps the point. See <a href="https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/split-shank-double-claw-solitaire-engagement-ring-in-14k-rose-gold-by-james-allen-item-315292?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">Split Shank Double Claw at $790</a>.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Bezel wrap</strong></td><td>Full metal surround</td><td>Lowest</td><td>Zero chip risk. Hides stone edge.</td></tr></tbody></table>

**Verdict:** Pick V-tip prongs minimum. If the ring listing does not spec V-tip or double-claw at the tips, call the retailer before ordering. Round prongs on marquise is the single most common cause of ruined stones at the one-year mark.

[![Marquise cut diamond solitaire engagement ring in 14k yellow gold with six prong V-tip setting shown head-on against white backdrop](/images/marquise-solitaire-engagement-ring-yellow-gold-six-prong.jpg)](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings?ShapeID=Marquise&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)

### Mistake 2 — Four Prongs Instead of Six or Ten

Four-prong settings put two prongs on each tip and nothing on the belly. The widest part of the stone — the two shoulders at the middle — sits unsupported. On a daily-wear ring, the shoulders are where impact lands first.

Six prongs (two at each tip, two at the shoulders) spread the clamping force. Ten prongs (two at each tip, three along each shoulder) wrap the whole stone. For stones above 1ct, four prongs is the mistake you pay for in year three.

<table class="dc-custom-table"><thead><tr><th>Prong Count</th><th>Coverage</th><th>Example Ring</th><th>Setting Price</th><th>What this means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Four prongs</strong></td><td>Tips only</td><td>Classic Four Prong Platinum</td><td><a href="https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/classic-four-prong-solitaire-item-194156?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">$1,180</a></td><td>Fine on sub-0.5ct. Mistake on 1ct+ marquise. Shoulders exposed.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Six prongs</strong></td><td>Tips + shoulders</td><td>Classic Six-Prong Platinum</td><td><a href="https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/classic-six-prong-solitaire-item-194157?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">$1,355</a></td><td>Our baseline for mid-size marquise. Industry best practice.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Ten prongs</strong></td><td>Full stone wrap</td><td>Ten Prong Platinum (JA)</td><td><a href="https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/ten-prong-solitaire-engagement-ring-in-platinum-by-james-allen-item-311240?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">$1,200</a></td><td>Insurance setup. Our top pick for peace of mind.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Bezel</strong></td><td>Continuous wrap</td><td>Comfort Fit Bezel Platinum</td><td>$1,250</td><td>Zero chip risk. Hides stone edge entirely.</td></tr></tbody></table>

**Verdict:** Six prongs is the minimum for 1ct+ marquise. Ten prongs is the right call above 2ct. The Ten Prong Solitaire by James Allen at $860 (gold) or $1,200 (platinum) is the single safest solitaire setting for a marquise stone in the entire certified market scan.

### Mistake 3 — 14k Yellow Gold Prongs Instead of Platinum

14k gold prongs bend over 5–7 years of daily wear. Bent prongs sit loose above the stone. Loose stones chip, scratch, or fall out. Platinum is 60% denser than gold and holds its shape across decades.

For a marquise above 1ct — any realistic engagement stone — the metal choice at the prongs is insurance against stone loss. The upgrade from 14k to platinum on the whole band costs $300–$600. The hybrid option (white gold band with platinum prongs only) costs $150–$300.

<table class="dc-custom-table"><thead><tr><th>Prong Metal</th><th>5-Year Deformation</th><th>Retighten Needed</th><th>What this means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>14k Yellow Gold</strong></td><td>Significant</td><td>Every 18 months</td><td>Softest option. Highest maintenance. Pass on marquise.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>18k Gold</strong></td><td>Moderate</td><td>Every 24 months</td><td>Better than 14k. Still softer than platinum.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Platinum</strong></td><td>Minimal</td><td>Every 36 months</td><td>Our pick. Lowest maintenance across 20+ years.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>White Gold + Platinum prongs</strong></td><td>Minimal at prongs</td><td>Every 36 months</td><td>Smart hybrid. Saves $300 on band without losing tip security.</td></tr></tbody></table>

**Verdict:** Platinum prongs minimum. On a budget, go with white gold band + platinum prongs — the retailer quotes the hybrid at order. Full yellow gold or rose gold prongs below 1ct is acceptable, above 1ct is a mistake.

### Mistake 4 — Skipping the 2-Year Retightening Check

Even platinum V-tip six-prong settings drift under normal wear. The drift is 10–30 microns a year — invisible to the naked eye and inside the stone's seat the first few years. By year five, the drift is enough that a hard impact loosens the stone. By year seven, the stone can rattle in the setting.

A 15-minute jeweller check at the counter catches this before stone loss. Most retailers that sold the ring include the service free for the ring's life.

<table class="dc-custom-table"><thead><tr><th>Inspection Interval</th><th>Stone Loss Risk</th><th>Cost</th><th>What this means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Never inspect</strong></td><td>Very High</td><td>$0 upfront</td><td>Stone loss within 7–10 years likely. Highest hidden cost.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Every 5 years</strong></td><td>Moderate</td><td>$0–$75</td><td>Catches most issues. Drift can still cause chips between visits.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Every 2 years</strong></td><td>Low</td><td>$0 if free from retailer</td><td>Industry best practice. Our standard.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Yearly</strong></td><td>Lowest</td><td>$0–$75</td><td>Overkill for most. Right for heavy wear.</td></tr></tbody></table>

**Verdict:** Set a calendar reminder for the 2-year mark the day the ring arrives. Call the retailer that sold it. If they do not offer free inspection, independent jewellers charge $25–$75 and finish in 15 minutes.

### Mistake 5 — Trellis Solitaire With Exposed Points

The trellis setting looks beautiful on round and oval stones. On a marquise, the trellis loops cross below the belly of the stone and leave the two sharp points sticking out past the metal entirely. Trellis is a round-and-oval design that was adapted badly for fancy shapes.

Basket-wrapped tips use a metal basket beneath the stone that extends up to the tips, forming a cup-like protection on each point. The marquise sits inside the basket. Both tips are protected.

<table class="dc-custom-table"><thead><tr><th>Setting Style</th><th>Tip Protection</th><th>Example</th><th>Price</th><th>What this means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Trellis (criss cross)</strong></td><td>Poor on marquise</td><td>Criss Cross Solitaire RG (JA)</td><td><a href="https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/criss-cross-solitaire-by-james-allen-item-311129?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">$1,650</a></td><td>Great on round. Avoid on marquise unless you upgrade tips to V-prongs.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Basket solitaire</strong></td><td>Full wrap</td><td>Solitaire with Intricate Basket YG</td><td><a href="https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/solitaire-engagement-ring-with-intricate-basket-in-14k-yellow-gold-by-james-allen-item-315036?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">$1,050</a></td><td>Our pick. Basket cups each tip.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cathedral solitaire</strong></td><td>Moderate</td><td>Tapered Cathedral YG</td><td><a href="https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/tapered-cathedral-solitaire-engagement-ring-in-14k-yellow-gold-item-203288?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">$1,240</a></td><td>Shoulders rise to meet stone. Pair with V-tip for marquise.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Knife-edge solitaire</strong></td><td>Depends on prong spec</td><td>Knife Edge Solitaire WG (JA)</td><td><a href="https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/knife-edge-solitaire-by-james-allen-item-316048?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">$925</a></td><td>Fine if V-tip + six prong.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Bezel</strong></td><td>Highest (full wrap)</td><td>Comfort Fit Bezel Platinum</td><td>$1,250</td><td>Zero chip risk.</td></tr></tbody></table>

**Verdict:** For a marquise solitaire, pick basket, cathedral, or bezel — not trellis. If you fall in love with a trellis, call the retailer and ask them to spec V-tip prongs on the two end positions. If they cannot, pass.

> **"The last five chipped marquise stones I graded for clients all came off trellis criss-cross solitaires. Every single one. The trellis is seductive — the band splits and the stone sits there alone in space. But on a marquise, that empty space around the tip is where the counter edge catches it. I ask clients to pick basket, cathedral, or ten-prong. Trellis is for round."** — Farzana Hasan, GIA Diamond Expert

## The Tip-Chip Threshold

**The Tip-Chip Threshold is the point where prong design stops being optional and starts being insurance.** Below 0.5ct, standard prongs work fine on marquise. At 0.5ct to 1ct, V-tip is advisable. At 1ct and above, V-tip is mandatory. At 2ct and above, V-tip + six prongs or ten prongs is the only honest answer.

<table class="dc-custom-table"><thead><tr><th>Marquise Carat</th><th>Tip Length (from centre)</th><th>Minimum Prong Spec</th><th>Suggested Ring</th><th>What this means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Under 0.5ct</strong></td><td>&lt; 4 mm</td><td>Four prongs OK</td><td><a href="https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/petite-solitaire-item-195639?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">Petite Solitaire YG $1,000</a></td><td>Below the Tip-Chip Threshold. Basic prongs acceptable.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>0.5ct to 1ct</strong></td><td>4–6 mm</td><td>V-tip, four prongs</td><td><a href="https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/classic-four-prong-solitaire-item-194156?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">Classic Four Prong Platinum $1,180</a></td><td>Transition zone. V-tip starts to matter.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>1ct to 2ct</strong></td><td>6–7.25 mm</td><td>V-tip + six prongs</td><td><a href="https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/classic-six-prong-solitaire-item-194157?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">Classic Six-Prong Platinum $1,355</a></td><td>Mandatory V-tip. Six prongs for shoulder support.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>2ct to 3ct</strong></td><td>7.25–7.5 mm</td><td>V-tip + six or ten prongs + platinum</td><td><a href="https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/ten-prong-solitaire-engagement-ring-in-platinum-by-james-allen-item-311240?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">Ten Prong Platinum $1,200</a></td><td>Full protection stack.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>3ct+</strong></td><td>7.5 mm+</td><td>Ten prongs + platinum</td><td><a href="https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/ten-prong-solitaire-engagement-ring-in-platinum-by-james-allen-item-311240?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">Ten Prong Platinum $1,200</a></td><td>Insurance setup. Add halo if possible.</td></tr></tbody></table>

**Verdict:** The Tip-Chip Threshold moves with carat. At 1ct — the most common marquise engagement stone — V-tip plus six prongs is where the real protection starts. Below that, basic solitaire works. Above that, every extra prong buys another decade of lifetime.

## The 10-Prong Insurance Rule

**The 10-Prong Insurance Rule is the no-chip baseline for anyone who wears their ring daily and does not want to think about it.** Ten prongs mean two at each tip (V-tip), three along each shoulder, and the whole stone sits inside a continuous metal frame. The visual is slightly bulkier than four or six prongs. The protection is the maximum any prong setting delivers short of a bezel.

The Ten Prong Solitaire in Platinum at $1,200 is the specific product that satisfies this rule. The gold versions at $860 (white / yellow / rose) deliver the same protection at a lower price point.

<table class="dc-custom-table"><thead><tr><th>Prong Setup</th><th>Chip Protection Score</th><th>Visual Weight</th><th>What this means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>4 prong round</strong></td><td>2/10</td><td>Slim</td><td>Lowest protection. Highest chip rate on marquise.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>4 prong V-tip</strong></td><td>5/10</td><td>Slim</td><td>Tips covered. Shoulders still exposed.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>6 prong V-tip</strong></td><td>7/10</td><td>Moderate</td><td>Industry baseline. Our minimum pick.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>10 prong V-tip</strong></td><td>9/10</td><td>Moderate-full</td><td>The 10-Prong Insurance Rule setup. Our top pick above 1.5ct.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Bezel</strong></td><td>10/10</td><td>Full wrap</td><td>Zero chip risk. Hides stone edge.</td></tr></tbody></table>

**Verdict:** Ten prongs is the sweet spot between slim visual and full protection. Below 1ct, six prongs is enough. Above 1.5ct, the 10-Prong Insurance Rule is the baseline.

[![Petite marquise cut diamond solitaire engagement ring in 14k yellow gold with slim band and V-tip prongs on a white background](/images/marquise-petite-solitaire-engagement-ring-yellow-gold.jpg)](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings?ShapeID=Marquise&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)

## The Trellis Trade-Off

**The Trellis Trade-Off is the single biggest aesthetic-vs-protection decision in a marquise solitaire purchase.** Trellis settings — where the shank splits and wraps around the stone basket in two decorative loops — look stunning on round, oval, and cushion shapes. On a marquise, they almost always leave the two sharp points exposed.

The reason is geometric. Trellis loops are symmetrical. On a round stone, every point of the girdle is equidistant from the loop. On a marquise, the belly sits close to the loops while the two tips extend past them.

<table class="dc-custom-table"><thead><tr><th>Shape</th><th>Trellis Fit</th><th>Protection on Trellis</th><th>What this means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Round</strong></td><td>Excellent</td><td>Full</td><td>Trellis is designed for round. All girdle points equidistant.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Oval</strong></td><td>Very good</td><td>Full</td><td>Oval girdle sits inside loops. Soft curves — no exposed points.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cushion</strong></td><td>Good</td><td>Full</td><td>Rounded corners stay inside loop coverage.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Pear</strong></td><td>Fair</td><td>Point exposed</td><td>One sharp point sticks past the loop.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Marquise</strong></td><td>Poor</td><td>Two points exposed</td><td>Both sharp tips extend past the loops. Trellis is a mistake here.</td></tr></tbody></table>

**Verdict:** The Trellis Trade-Off is real — the look is beautiful, the protection is poor on marquise. If you love the trellis aesthetic, pick an oval or round instead. See [oval vs marquise diamond](/oval-vs-marquise-diamond) for shape alternatives. If marquise is non-negotiable, go basket, cathedral, or ten-prong.

