> **"Most of my clients come in asking about the diamond grade. I always redirect them. Save $200 on the stone grade. Spend it on the setting. A G-colour, VS2, 1.5ct lab in a $1,200 platinum ten-prong beats a D-colour, VVS1, 1.5ct lab in a $600 four-prong gold ring every single day of the year. The grade upgrade is invisible. The setting upgrade is forever."** — Farzana Hasan, GIA Diamond Expert

## Solitaire vs Halo vs Three-Stone for Marquise

A solitaire is one of three main setting families for marquise. The other two — halo and three-stone — change the maths. Quick comparison:

<table class="dc-custom-table"><thead><tr><th>Setting Family</th><th>Setting Cost</th><th>Tip Protection</th><th>Visual</th><th>What this means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Solitaire</strong></td><td>$790–$2,850</td><td>Depends on prongs</td><td>Classic, slim</td><td>Our pick for traditional buyers. 50+ settings in the main table above.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Halo</strong></td><td>$1,255–$5,985</td><td>High (halo wraps belly)</td><td>Bigger-looking stone</td><td>Full comparison in <a href="/marquise-halo-engagement-ring">halo guide</a>.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Three-Stone</strong></td><td>$1,970–$4,790</td><td>Moderate</td><td>Dramatic, meaningful</td><td>Side stones distract from tip chip risk. See <a href="/marquise-three-stone-engagement-ring">three-stone guide</a>.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Bezel</strong></td><td>$990–$1,550</td><td>Highest (full wrap)</td><td>Modern, minimalist</td><td>Zero chip risk. Hides stone edge.</td></tr></tbody></table>

**Verdict:** The solitaire gives you the cleanest classic look. The halo gives you the biggest visual stone. The three-stone gives you the most meaningful ring. For pure tip protection, bezel wins — but you lose the stone's edge visibility.

