## How to Verify a Marquise Solitaire Before You Buy

Three checks every ring must pass before you commit.

**Check 1 — Confirm V-tip prongs at the end positions.** Open the retailer listing. The 360° ring video should show two prongs per tip, wrapping from both sides. If the listing says "four-prong" or shows round prongs on the tips, upgrade at the order or walk.

**Check 2 — Confirm prong count is six minimum.** Six prongs = two at each tip, one at each shoulder. Ten prongs = full wrap. Four prongs is a mistake on marquise above 0.5ct.

**Check 3 — Confirm metal is platinum at the prongs minimum.** Full platinum band is best. White gold band + platinum prongs is the budget compromise. [IGI](https://www.igi.org/) and [GIA](https://4cs.gia.edu/) both certify loose stones only — the setting metal is on the retailer's listing, not the certificate.

I run these three checks before I quote a client. The stone has to pass the first two from the certificate scan. The setting is the third — and often the difference between a ring that lasts 50 years and one that chips at 18 months.

